-\subsection*{\toolname co-phases SNVs, indels, SVs and methylation in one graph and scores every phased variant}
-\toolname resolves haplotypes in three stages (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}). All heterozygous evidence carried by the same long reads, comprising SNVs, small indels, SVs and allele-specific 5mC sites called by the built-in \texttt{modcall} module, enters a single weighted phasing graph per chromosome. Within that graph each observation is weighted by base quality and by the reliability of its sequence context, which down-weights homopolymers, tandem repeats and copy-number-altered regions (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}a,b). Each phased variant also receives a phasing-uncertainty score, and a graph neural network inspects a local window around every variant whose uncertainty is high (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}c). Variants predicted to be misphased are unphased instead of flipped (referred to below as correction), and any haplotype block that becomes disconnected is split. Genotypes are thus preserved, and no new phase assignment is introduced. The outputs are phased VCFs for every variant class and haplotagged alignments (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}d).
