-\subsection*{The correction transfers to PacBio HiFi data without retraining}
-The network was trained only on nanopore data. Applied without retraining to SNV-only phasing of HG002 PacBio HiFi data at 10--50$\times$, it removed 19--28\% of the switch errors of \toolname (1,328 to 1,070 at 10$\times$; 1,165 to 862 at 50$\times$), against 31--33\% on nanopore data, at a cost of 0.09--0.10 percentage points of phased SNVs and 1--2\% of block N50 (Fig.~\ref{fig:hifi} and Supplementary Table~11). Corrected \toolname made 2.5--3.5-fold fewer switch errors than \whatshap (2,618--2,778 at every coverage) and 2.0--2.5-fold fewer without correction, with a lower Hamming distance at every coverage (0.78--1.05\% versus 1.03--1.66\%). Block N50 was within 3\% of that of \whatshap at every coverage, and \whatshap phased 0.4--0.7 percentage points more SNVs. Unlike on nanopore data, the switch errors of \whatshap did not decrease with coverage, whereas those of \toolname fell from 1,328 to 1,102 between 10 and 40$\times$ before correction. HiFi blocks were 4.6 times shorter than nanopore blocks at matched coverage (N50 0.55 versus 2.51~Mb at 50$\times$), as expected from the shorter reads\todo{The HiFi call sets are nearly identical at every coverage (2,367,609--2,367,619 heterozygous SNVs from 10 to 50$\times$; Supplementary Table~7), which suggests that variants were called once and the same calls phased at every down-sampled coverage, unlike the nanopore runs. Confirm, and state the HiFi data source, caller and procedure in Methods; if one call set was used, the phased fraction at 10$\times$ (87\%, against 78\% on nanopore) is not comparable between platforms}.
-
-\subsection*{SNVs unphased by correction are enriched outside assembly-assessable regions}
-To characterize what correction removes, we classified every SNV that \toolname phased and correction subsequently unphased (SNV-only phasing, replicate~1) against the v5.0q benchmark VCF (Fig.~\ref{fig:unphased}a). Correction unphased 59,000--64,000 SNVs at every coverage. Of these, 81\% at 10$\times$ and 93\% at 60$\times$ were absent from the benchmark VCF, 3--5\% were homozygous in it and 14\% at 10$\times$ falling to 4.0\% at 60$\times$ (8,673 and 2,411 SNVs) were heterozygous in it\todo{The classification counts benchmark VCF records outside the benchmark BED, whereas \texttt{compare} scores only inside it: \texttt{compare} puts the loss of phased benchmark SNVs at 60$\times$ at 2,055 (2,193,366 before and 2,191,311 after correction, Supplementary Data~1), and the assembly analysis below finds only 549 unphased SNVs inside the benchmark BED, of which 413 are benchmark variants. The two analyses also count different totals at 60$\times$ (60,267 here, 56,076 in the assembly analysis of the same replicate). State the matching rule and SNV filter of each and reconcile the totals}.
-
-Because the benchmark VCF is derived from the T2T-HG002 assembly, absence from it means either that the assembly carries no variant at the site or that the assembly does not align to GRCh38 there. We separated the two by classifying the 56,076 SNVs unphased at 60$\times$ by the status of the T2T-HG002 v1.1 assembly at each site (Fig.~\ref{fig:unphased}b and Supplementary Table~12). Only 11.8\% of them lay where both assembled haplotypes align one-to-one to GRCh38, against 92.6\% of a random sample of 50,000 SNVs that stayed phased (odds ratio 94). The remaining 88.2\% lay where the assembly does not align one-to-one and where no truth set derived from it can therefore assess them. The share outside the alignment was higher among unphased SNVs on every autosome (Fig.~\ref{fig:unphased}c and Supplementary Table~12), and the unassessed sites clustered in pericentromeric satellite sequence, most densely at chr11:51--54~Mb, chr12:35--36~Mb and chr18:15--20~Mb. Of the 6,601 unphased SNVs inside the alignment, 3,421 (52\%) had no assembly VCF record at the same coordinate and 3,180 had one\todo{v5.0q is derived from the same assembly; this analysis extends coverage beyond the benchmark BED; it is not independent confirmation. The dipcall BED is the raw alignment and does not exclude the assembly errors that the benchmark curation removes. Rebuild this analysis before submission from one site-level table: autosomes only (chrX and chrY are currently included); a control drawn from SNVs that stayed phased, excluding the removed ones (the current background is sampled from all SNVs phased before correction, \texttt{run\_t2t\_validation.sh}:75); and an allele-aware comparison with the assembly VCF that handles overlapping complex variants (the current test matches the start coordinate only, line~104). Derive every panel of Fig.~6 from that table}. Correction therefore withdraws phase mostly from SNVs outside the regions in which the assembly-derived truth set can assess phase, and about half of those inside lack an assembly variant record at the queried coordinate.
-
-Cross-tabulating the unphased SNVs by read depth, phasing informativeness and phasing entropy (Supplementary Fig.~13) showed that benchmark heterozygotes were removed mostly at normal depth with weak phasing evidence (61--81\%), whereas benchmark-absent calls shifted towards low depth as coverage increased (12.5\% at 10$\times$ to 58.6\% at 60$\times$), as expected in hard-to-map regions where reads are lost to alternative loci. Among the benchmark heterozygotes removed, 43\% at 10$\times$ and 22\% at 60$\times$ had a phasing entropy of exactly 0, that is, unanimous but sparse votes or, at the first variant of a block, no votes at all (Methods), compared with 17\% of benchmark-absent calls; a further 21--25\% of benchmark heterozygotes had near-equivocal votes\todo{these prevalences were read from the earlier Fig.~6b,c (pptx), now Supplementary Fig.~13; replace with exact values and regenerate the figure}. The vote balance at the variant itself would therefore have missed most of the removed heterozygotes. Clustering within 50~bp and low genotype quality were present in 46--49\% of benchmark-absent calls at every coverage and were far less frequent among benchmark heterozygotes, consistent with variant-caller artefacts.
-
-\subsection*{SNVs left unphased by one tool are enriched in the other tool's switch-error intervals}
-At each coverage examined (10, 30 and 60$\times$), corrected \toolname left more heterozygous SNV calls unphased than \whatshap (210,683 versus 142,313 at 60$\times$, among calls present in both output VCFs), but the two sets overlapped only partly (Fig.~\ref{fig:unphased}d). At 60$\times$, 88,692 SNVs were left unphased by both tools, 121,991 by \toolname alone (99,813 at the phasing stage and 22,178 by the correction) and 53,621 by \whatshap alone. To relate the two sets to phasing accuracy, we measured the share of each that fell inside genomic intervals containing switch disagreements between the other tool and the truth set (Fig.~\ref{fig:unphased}e); this measures regional co-location, not whether each site was phased correctly. Every set was enriched there, and the enrichment grew with coverage on both sides: the SNVs \toolname alone left unphased at the phasing stage were 4.2-fold enriched in \whatshap's switch-error intervals at 10$\times$ and 10.4-fold at 60$\times$, those removed by the correction 4.5- and 5.8-fold, and those \whatshap alone left unphased were 2.0- and 9.5-fold enriched in the intervals of \toolname. Sites left unphased by either tool were thus enriched in intervals containing switch disagreements in the other tool. The two tools also left SNVs unphased for different reasons (Supplementary Fig.~12). At 60$\times$ the sites \whatshap alone left unphased had a median depth of 18$\times$, consistent with too little evidence to phase, whereas those left unphased by \toolname alone had a depth distribution similar to that of all sites, with 15.6\% at the depth cap of the variant caller, a pile-up typical of reads mismapped from paralogous sequence, where evidence is abundant but conflicting\todo{the median depths and the zero-entropy share among \whatshap-only sites (57.6\%) are quoted from JHL's commit message of 2026-09-27 and are not in any committed table; add the per-set summary to Supplementary Data~1}.
